## Context
Child F of tracker 1045, at the author's request on 2026-10-21 ("drop the
pull_request trigger"). The forge ruleset requiring checks is disabled; the gate
is local (children A and B). `.github/workflows/CI.yml` still runs the full
workflow on every push to every pull request (74 of the latest 100 runs were
`pull_request` events, 26 were pushes to `main`), at no cash cost (public repo)
but with no reader. The push-to-`main` run is kept: it is the ex-post record of
when `main` went red, on a second environment (a clean forge runner, not padme),
and the sweep of child B can read it.

Not a two-line edit, found when preparing it: `scripts/local-ci.sh` and
`scripts/local-ci-negative-controls.sh` run the workflow with
`act pull_request`, so removing the `pull_request:` trigger makes `act` find no
workflow. And the job `cross-pr-ticket-collision` runs only on a pull_request
event (it needs the PR number and the base ref), so it would stop running.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `CI.yml` triggers are `push` to `main` and `workflow_dispatch`; no
      `pull_request` trigger on the full workflow.
- [ ] `scripts/local-ci.sh` and `scripts/local-ci-negative-controls.sh` keep
      working: they invoke the workflow through the surviving trigger, and their
      tests pass (`tests/test_local_ci_*.py`), with the negative controls re-run
      where docker is available (say which ran and which were skipped).
- [ ] `cross-pr-ticket-collision` keeps running on pull requests: moved to its own
      small workflow file triggered by `pull_request` (it takes seconds), with the
      job removed from `CI.yml`; its local run still works. State the choice
      (separate workflow vs. fold the check into the gate's check set) in the PR.
- [ ] The PR body says how to turn the forge CI back on per pull request.
- [ ] No other workflow behaviour changes; PR under the 15-file breaker.
